# ==== A16 [analysis]: Rescale the pooled accuracy ... ====
# Summary:
#   Rescale the pooled accuracy (.42 at six alternatives) onto the null-.50 index pi =
#   P(k-1)/(1+P(k-2)) = .78; rescale the Candy Smell Test anosmia cut-off (.57 at four
#   alternatives) to pi = .80; and convert the grand macro-AUC (.68) to d = sqrt(2)*z(AUC) =
#   0.66 and r = d/sqrt(d^2+4) = .31.
# Source quote:
#   "Our pooled .42 at six alternatives becomes p = .78 ... the anosmia cut-off of the Candy
#   Smell Test, a four-alternative retronasal test (Renner et al., 2009), rescales to p =
#   .80. ... On the discrimination metric, the grand macro-AUC of .68 corresponds under an
#   equal-variance binormal model to d = 0.66 and an effect-size-equivalent r = .31."
# Inputs: inputs/gummibear_trials.csv
# Depends on: A08, A14, L01
# Produces: T066, T067, T068, T069
def rescale_pi(P, k):
    return P * (k - 1) / (1 + P * (k - 2))
T066 = rescale_pi(k_all / 100.0, 6)       # P=.42, k=6 -> .7836 -> .78
T067 = rescale_pi(0.57, 4)                # Candy Smell Test anosmia cut-off -> .7991 -> .80
# ASSUMPTION: the paper converts the rounded grand macro-AUC (.68) to d; computing from
# the unrounded value (0.676) gives d = .65. To reproduce the paper's quoted d = .66 and
# r = .31 we use the AUC rounded to two decimals, matching the value the paper reports.
_auc_d = round(T060, 2)
_z = stats.norm.ppf(_auc_d)
T068 = math.sqrt(2.0) * _z               # d = 0.6614 -> 0.66
T069 = T068 / math.sqrt(T068 ** 2 + 4)   # r = 0.314 -> 0.31
